# System Architecture

The complete system will look like:

                         CPU / Master
                              │
                              ▼
                       Wishbone Bus
                              │
                              ▼
                       Address Decoder
                              │
              ┌───────────────┼───────────────┐
              │               │               │
              ▼               ▼               ▼
             RAM             GPIO            UART
          0x0000            0x1000          0x2000

The CPU-like Master generates transactions.

The Wishbone bus transports the transaction.

The address decoder determines which peripheral should receive it.

The selected peripheral performs the operation and returns an acknowledgement and, when necessary, read data.

---

# Address Map

The system uses the following address map:

| Address           | Peripheral | Description            |
|-------------------|------------|------------------------|
| "0x0000 - 0x00FF" | RAM        | General-purpose memory |
| "0x1000 - 0x10FF" | GPIO       | GPIO output register   |
| "0x2000 - 0x20FF" | UART       | UART transmit register |
| "0x3000 - 0x30FF" | Reserved   | Future timer           |

The timer address is reserved for a future project.

It should not be implemented yet.

---

# Address Decoder

The address decoder is one of the most important components in this project.

Its job is to examine the address from the Master and determine which peripheral should be selected.

Conceptually:

                    Address
                       │
                       ▼
                ┌─────────────┐
                │   Address   │
                │   Decoder   │
                └──────┬──────┘
                       │
         ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
         ▼             ▼             ▼
        RAM           GPIO          UART
      0x0000         0x1000        0x2000


For example:


ADR = 0x0010


selects:


RAM


while:


ADR = 0x1000


selects:


GPIO


and:


ADR = 0x2000


selects:


UART


---

# RAM Access

RAM occupies:


0x0000 - 0x00FF


A write to an address inside this range is sent to RAM.

For example:


Address = 0x0010
Data    = 0x42
Write   = 1


The expected result is:


RAM[0x0010] = 0x42


A subsequent read should return:


0x42


The architecture is:


CPU
 │
 │ 0x0010
 ▼
Wishbone
 │
 ▼
Address Decoder
 │
 └──────► RAM


---

# GPIO Access

GPIO is mapped to:


0x1000


A write to "0x1000" updates the GPIO output register.

For example:


Address = 0x1000
Data    = 0xAA
Write   = 1


results in:


GPIO = 0xAA


Binary:


10101010


The architecture is:


CPU
 │
 │ 0x1000
 │ 0xAA
 ▼
Wishbone
 │
 ▼
Address Decoder
 │
 └──────► GPIO
             │
             ▼
          GPIO[7:0]


---

# UART Access

The existing UART from the "uart-from-scratch" project is mapped to:


0x2000


A write to this address represents a UART transmit operation.

For example:


Address = 0x2000
Data = 0x41
Write = 1


"0x41" represents:


A


The transaction should therefore pass the value to the existing UART transmitter.

The UART itself is not rebuilt.

The architecture becomes:


CPU
 │
 │ 0x2000
 │ 0x41
 ▼
Wishbone
 │
 ▼
Address Decoder
 │
 └──────► UART MMIO Interface
               │
               ▼
        Existing UART TX


---

# UART Integration

The existing UART contains the logic required for serial transmission.

The new MMIO system only needs to determine when the CPU is writing to the UART address.

Conceptually:


if address == 0x2000
and write == 1
then
    send data to UART


The existing UART handles the actual:

- Baud generation
- TX state machine
- Serial output

This keeps the MMIO project focused on SoC architecture rather than rebuilding the UART.

