# Important Design Principle

The peripherals should not need to know the entire SoC architecture.

For example, GPIO should not need to know:


Where RAM is located
Where UART is located
How the CPU works


GPIO only needs to respond when it is selected.

The address decoder handles the system-level routing.

This creates a modular architecture:


Address Decoder
      │
      ├──► RAM
      │
      ├──► GPIO
      │
      └──► UART


This makes it easier to add more peripherals later.

---

# Why This Matters for the SoC

The final Linux computer will need a similar structure.

Eventually the architecture will look more like:


                         RISC-V CPU
                              │
                              ▼
                            Bus
                              │
                     ┌────────┴────────┐
                     │                 │
                   Memory          Peripherals
                     │                 │
              ┌──────┴──────┐     ┌────┴────┐
              ▼             ▼     ▼         ▼
             ROM           RAM   UART      GPIO
                                  │
                                  ▼
                                Timer


Linux will eventually interact with hardware peripherals through this type of architecture.

The exact bus and peripheral arrangement may change when the final SoC is implemented, but the fundamental concept remains the same.

---

# Connection to Linux

Memory-Mapped I/O is particularly important for the Linux project.

Linux needs to communicate with hardware.

For example:


Linux program
     ↓
Linux driver
     ↓
CPU instruction
     ↓
Memory-mapped address
     ↓
SoC bus
     ↓
Peripheral


A program may eventually cause the CPU to access a hardware register.

For example:


Write 1
   ↓
GPIO register
   ↓
FPGA pin
   ↓
LED


The software sees an address.

The hardware interprets that address as a peripheral register.

This is one of the key connections between software and hardware in an SoC.

