# Reflection

This project was the first point where the individual components from the previous projects started to form a real SoC architecture.

Instead of treating RAM, GPIO and UART as completely separate systems, they are now accessed through the same Wishbone bus.

The most important concept I learned was the address decoder.

The CPU-like Master does not need to know how each peripheral works. It only needs to access the correct address.

For example:

0x0010 → RAM
0x1000 → GPIO
0x2000 → UART

The address decoder determines which peripheral should receive the transaction.

This makes the system modular and makes it possible to add more peripherals later.

---

# Problems

One of the main challenges was making sure that only the correct peripheral responds to a transaction.

If multiple peripherals respond to the same address, the system could produce incorrect read data or multiple acknowledgements.

Another challenge was integrating the existing UART into the new architecture without rebuilding it.

The UART already contains its own transmission logic, so the new MMIO system only needs to provide the correct data and start the transmission when the UART address is accessed.

Understanding the difference between the bus interface and the actual peripheral logic was therefore important.

---

# What I Would Improve

The current system is intentionally simple.

Possible improvements include:

- More sophisticated address decoding
- More registers per peripheral
- Timer peripheral
- Interrupt support
- Multiple bus masters
- More realistic error handling
- Larger address spaces

These features can be added as the SoC becomes more advanced.

